Honest question for {{area}} landlords: what are you paying all-in to have a property managed, once renewal fees, inspection fees and "admin" are added?

I ask because I'm the {{area}} Local Partner for Hello New Home and the fee is 12% of rent, capped at £150 a month, no VAT, with nothing added. Setup is waived if you're switching an existing tenancy, and it's 30 days' notice to leave.

Not posting to argue with anyone's agent. If you want a side-by-side on your own numbers, head office does a free written rent review within one working day: {{area_url}}

Most management agreements tie you in for 12 months, sometimes with a fee to leave early. Ours is 30 days' notice, no exit fee, from day one.

We can say that because the fee is published in full and the service is the same every month: rent in, statement out, repairs answered, inspections done, certificates booked before they are due. If we stop doing that, you leave. We would rather keep you because it is working than because of a clause.

If your current agreement has months to run, that is fine; we don't ask anyone to break one. Send head office a photo of it and they will tell you your earliest clean exit date, free: {{area_url}}

From 15 December 2026 the private rented sector database opens in England, region by region. You register yourself and each property, pay a fee, and keep the entry current. Letting without an entry becomes an offence once your region's deadline has passed.

What it means in practice for {{area}}: another date on the calendar, and a public record that councils and tenants can check. If you already keep gas, electrical, EPC and deposit paperwork in order, registration is an afternoon.

If you'd rather not keep a calendar at all, that is what Full Management is for, 12% capped at £150 a month. Either way the free checklist on the area page has the dates: {{area_url}}

If you let a home in {{area}}, your tenant is a contract-holder, that is what Wales calls a tenant, and the agreement is an occupation contract. You have 14 days from the start of the contract to give them the written statement.

Miss it and two things happen. The contract-holder can claim compensation for every day it is late, and you cannot serve a landlord's notice to end the contract until six months after you finally hand it over. A missing piece of paper on day one can cost you a year later.

Head office issues the written statement on every managed property before the keys are handed over, and I do the move-in here in {{area}}. If you self-manage, the free Landlord Compliance Checklist on the area page has the Renting Homes (Wales) Act dates on one side: {{area_url}}

If your {{area}} property takes paying guests for stays of 31 nights or less, you need to be on the Welsh visitor accommodation register with the Welsh Revenue Authority. The scheme opened in October 2026 and existing hosts have until 31 March 2027. Registration is free; missing it is not optional.

One more figure to watch. The 182-day rule: available to let 252 days a year and actually let 182, or the property moves from business rates to council tax, and some councils charge a premium on second homes.

Full SA Management with Hello New Home includes the register admin and a 182-day tracker on your monthly statement. Or start with the free appraisal and legality check for your address, which includes the registration steps: {{area_url}}

If you are looking for a home in {{area}}, this is what you should expect from any agent, including us.

A holding deposit never more than one week's rent, taken only when you have been told the terms. Your deposit protected within 30 days, with the certificate sent to you. A written agreement you can read before you commit, not one handed over on moving day. Gas and electrical certificates shown to you, and alarms that work. A repairs line that answers at 2am as well as 2pm. A real person for viewings and move-in; in {{area}} that is me.

If you are being asked for more than that, ask why. If you want to register what you are looking for and where, head office will call you when something fits: {{area_url}}

Three streets I walked this week, because that is most of the job.

{{place_1}}: two boards from two different agents on the same terrace, which usually means two landlords paying two different percentages for the same service. {{place_2}}: a flat above a shop that has been to let since {{month}} began; a week's void on a {{area}} rent is worth more than most people think. {{place_3}}: a new gas engineer who has agreed to do our checks locally, which means a boiler failure here gets someone from here.

If you own a rental on any of those streets and would like an honest figure for what it should let at this month, the rent review is free and takes head office a working day: {{area_url}}

A small ask, once. If Hello New Home has let, managed or found you a home in {{area}} and it went the way you would want, a sentence on Google about what actually happened, how fast the repairs line answered, whether the figure we gave you was the one it let at, helps the next landlord or tenant here choose.

If it did not go well, please tell me or head office first, by message or on {{phone}}, and we will put it right before you write anything. We ask everyone, we do not offer anything for a review, and we answer every one.

The review link is on the area page: {{area_url}}
